        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "After having looked closer into this, I am mostly positive that this is not a 'mini.statusline' issue. Here is the outline:\r\n- 'mini.statusline'  sets window-local 'statusline' options to show either `content.active` or `content.inactive` [inside `(Win,Buf)(Enter,Leave)` events](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/f54410c7f866f51711118fd82274043cf731b63d/lua/mini/statusline.lua#L468) - active statusline is shown on enter, inactive - on leave. This is reasonable and proved to work well.\r\n- On the other hand, when closing 'neo-tree' window with `Neotree toggle focus`, there is no `WinEnter` event for the window which is focused after that. **But** there is such event triggered if window is closed with `:quit` (which caused initial confusion in this issue).\r\n- This is an indicator that in 'neo-tree.nvim' code implementing `:Neotree toggle focus` previous window is focused at the time when events (or only `WinEnter` in particular) can not be triggered. Usually it is either:\r\n    - Command is executed with `noautocmd`.\r\n    - `eventignore` is such that both `WinEnter` and `BufEnter` are ignored.\r\n    - Window is set inside an autocommand which is configured without `nested = true` (or `++nested` in Vimscript). This seems like the most probable cause. \r\n- I tried to find a fix in 'neo-tree.nvim', but after about 20 minutes I was not even sure if I found a place where `toggle close` command is actually executed. So decided to just create an issue, but there is already a similar one: nvim-neo-tree/neo-tree.nvim#1211.\r\n\r\nClosing as this is most certainly not a 'mini.statusline' issue.",
